1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
"I'm knackered - have you got any tips for a good night's sleep?"
"我累坏了——你有什么睡个好觉的妙招吗？"

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:11,890
Not only is sleep a common feature of our small talk, we've also spent decades trying to understand and explain it.
睡眠不仅是我们闲聊的常见话题，几十年来我们也一直在试图理解和解释它。

3
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:16,920
After all, we spend on average 26 years of our lives asleep.
毕竟，我们一生中平均有26年的时间在睡觉。

4
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:19,820
So, what do we know?
那么，我们了解到了什么呢？

5
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:23,600
Here are the highlights of the last decade of research.
以下是过去十年研究的亮点。

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:33,350
One of the most intriguing developments is in the study of lucid dreaming – it seems it's possible to communicate with people while they're sleeping.
最引人入胜的进展之一是清醒梦研究——似乎可以在人们睡觉时与他们交流。

7
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:45,130
In a study called 'Real-time dialogue between experimenters and dreamers during REM sleep', researchers asked questions to participants in a lucid dream.
在一项名为"快速眼动睡眠期间实验者与做梦者之间的实时对话"的研究中，研究人员向处于清醒梦状态的参与者提问。

8
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:50,350
The dreamers signalled the answers using pre-agreed eye or facial movements.
做梦者使用预先约定的眼部或面部动作来示意答案。

9
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:57,330
They were accurate, suggesting they could access their cognitive functions such as working memory while sleeping.
他们的回答是准确的，这表明他们能够在睡眠时调用认知功能，如工作记忆。

10
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:04,140
This interaction between dreamworld and reality has brought excitement to the sleep science community.
这种梦境与现实之间的互动让睡眠科学界都很兴奋。

11
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:08,280
Sleep can also help our memory and problem-solving.
睡眠还能帮助我们记忆和解决问题。

12
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:17,950
A 2023 review of research by Denis and Cairney noted that brain regions that are used to learn new things, are reactivated during sleep.
丹尼斯和凯尔尼在2023年的一项研究综述中指出，用于学习新事物的大脑区域在睡眠期间会被重新激活。

13
00:01:17,950 --> 00:01:27,870
That means while we're sleeping, our brains replay our memories of what happened during the day, which is believed to be crucial for memory retention.
这意味着当我们睡觉时，我们的大脑会回放白天发生事件的记忆，这被认为对保持记忆至关重要。

14
00:01:27,870 --> 00:01:36,440
And, if you've ever felt it was best to sleep on a tricky problem and solve it in the morning, you might have scientific backing!
而且，如果你曾经觉得最好先把难题放一放，睡一觉，第二天早上再解决，你可能是有科学依据的！

15
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:49,180
A 2019 study by Sanders and colleagues found that people were more likely to solve a tricky problem the next day if the problem had been activated in their brains during sleep.
桑德斯及其同事在2019年的一项研究发现，如果一个棘手的问题在睡眠期间被激活，人们更有可能在第二天解决它。

16
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:54,070
So, if you're stuck, rest up and return to it in the morning.
所以，如果你陷入困境，休息一下，第二天早上再回来解决。

17
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:58,500
What if you're unlucky enough to suffer with sleep deprivation?
如果你不幸遭受睡眠不足呢？

18
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:15,250
Unfortunately, you may be at higher risk of negative moods and worse emotional regulation, according to a 2021 meta-analysis called 'The effect of sleep deprivation and restriction on mood, emotion, and emotion regulation'.
不幸的是，根据2021年一项名为"睡眠不足和限制对情绪、情感和情绪调节的影响"的汇总分析，你可能面临更高的负面情绪风险和更差的情绪调节能力。

19
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:20,220
The good news is there are plenty of tips to help you with your sleep hygiene.
好消息是，有很多方法可以帮助你改善睡眠卫生。

20
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:33,200
The UK's National Health Service recommends having a set time to start winding down, meditation before bed, which they call 'beditation', and avoiding caffeine before bed.
英国国家医疗服务体系建议设定一个固定的时间开始放松，睡前冥想（他们称之为"床想"），并避免睡前摄入咖啡因。
